Note: This specification is under active development and therefore incomplete. If you're looking for an overview of the proposal, please refer to the [Explainer](https://github.com/WICG/local-peer-to-peer/blob/main/EXPLAINER.md).

Introduction {#intro}
=====================

*This section is non-normative.*

The Local Peer-to-Peer API aims to give browsers the means to communicate directly, without the aid of a server in the middle. It is designed to enable this communication within the confines of a [=local communication medium=].

Many modern Web security measures rely on the presence of naming, signaling and certificate authorities. Local use-cases where these authorities are not readily available have started lagging behind in user experience or are not supported altogether. The Local Peer-to-Peer API aims to bring back first-class support for local communication use-cases while working within the same strict user-friendliness, security and privacy requirements.

Examples of potential uses of this API include: Collaboration tools that work during an internet outage or emergency situations, connecting to your NAS, your home security system, your robotic assistant doing the dishes or your GPU farm in the basement that's running your personalized virtual assistant. See also [Use Cases](https://github.com/WICG/local-peer-to-peer/blob/main/EXPLAINER.md#use-cases).

This specification aims to strike a balance between creating a powerful new building block for developers and providing a seamless, secure and privacy preserving experience for browser users. As an example: while the API doesn't provide raw socket access, it does aim to give developers the flexibility to innovate on top by providing a persistent, two-way communication channel with little overhead.

The API is designed to be backed by an authenticated, streams-based transport. It is defined against the abstract operations of [[#transport-abstraction]], which a [=transport binding=] maps onto a concrete protocol. As a commitment to an open standards-based implementation path, this specification defines a binding to the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] in [[#osp-binding]]. Other bindings are anticipated, for example WebTransport over QUIC with peers authenticated on the local network as explored by the IETF [SETTLE](https://datatracker.ietf.org/wg/settle/about/) working group.

Terminology {#terminology}
======================================================

The term <dfn>local communication medium</dfn> refers to a communication medium that provides a local, direct communication channel between the communicating devices. It carries IP between the devices, directly or through a local network, for example a wired or wireless local area network or Wi-Fi Direct. Discovery and authentication may also use other channels, such as Bluetooth Low Energy, NFC or a QR code. This spec will not mandate which underlying technologies must be supported.

The term <dfn>local communication</dfn> refers to data communication over the [=local communication medium=].

The term <dfn>communication medium topology</dfn> refers to the composition of the [=local communication medium=] which may include sensitive information such as IP or other address information or the number of devices connected to the medium.

Permission Policy Integration {#permission-policy-integration}
======================================================

The Local Peer-to-Peer API defines a [=policy-controlled feature=] identified by the token "local-peer-to-peer". Its [=default allowlist=] is `"self"`.

Workers (dedicated and shared) adhere to the permission policy set by their owning document(s):
<ul>
  <li>
    Dedicated workers can be created from other workers, in which case the permission policy of the first owning document (in case of a dedicated worker) or owning documents (in case of a shared worker) up the owner chain will be used.
  </li>
  <li>
    Shared workers often have multiple owning documents as they can be obtained by other documents with the [=same origin=]. In this case, all owning documents must be [=allowed to use=] the [=policy-controlled feature=] defined by this specification.
  </li>
</ul>

Note: There has been discussion on allowing setting permission policy directly on a worker on creation, in which case that would have to be consulted as well.
 
The [=policy-controlled feature/default allowlist=] of `"self"` allows usage in same-origin nested frames but prevents third-party content from using the feature. Third-party content usage can be selectively enabled by adding `allow="local-peer-to-peer"` attribute to the frame container element:

<pre class="example" title= "Enabling Local Peer-to-Peer API on third-party content" highlight='js'>
&lt;iframe src="https://third-party.com" allow="local-peer-to-peer"/&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;
</pre>

Alternatively, the Local Peer-to-Peer API can be disabled completely by specifying the permissions policy in a HTTP response header:

<pre class="example" title="Permission Policy over HTTP" highlight='js'>
Permissions-Policy: local-peer-to-peer=()
</pre>

See [[!PERMISSIONS-POLICY]] for more details.

Peer Management {#peer-management}
==================================

Note: This section and its subsections use RFC 2119 terminology in a relaxed manner. These sections will be converted into well-defined algorithmic normative prose informed by further implementation experience. For now, this relaxed description better allows for rapid prototyping.

The user agent is in charge of managing peers.

A <dfn>peer</dfn> is an equal participant in the [=local communication=]. These nodes can communicate without the need for a central coordination by a server.

A user agent has an associated <dfn>local peer-to-peer manager</dfn> in charge of managing [=peers=]. Its responsibility is to [=start local peer discovery=], [=establish a session|establish sessions=] and [=acquire a local peer grant=] on a per-origin basis. This is done to avoid exposing information about the [=communication medium topology=].

Local state {#known-peers}
--------------------------

The [=local peer-to-peer manager=] has:

: <dfn>local trust store</dfn>
:: A [=/set=] of [=trust anchors=], see [[#peer-authentication]].
: <dfn>known peers map</dfn>
:: An [=/ordered map=] from [=peer/peer identity=] to <dfn>known peer</dfn>: a [=peer=] the user agent has had an [=protocol/authenticated=] session with.
: <dfn>peer grants</dfn>
:: An [=/ordered map=] from [=/origin=] to the [=/set=] of [=peer/peer identities=] that the origin may use.

A [=known peer=] records the <dfn for="known peer">anchor key</dfn> and <dfn for="known peer">subject</dfn> it last chained to on an [=protocol/authenticated=] session, or null. Whether it is [=/authenticated=] is not stored: without a live session, it is evaluated on these, against the [=local trust store=]. So removing a [=trust anchor=] can tell which peers are no longer [=/authenticated=].

The state is changed only by the operations below. How it is stored is [=implementation-defined=].

Other specifications can define <dfn>trust anchor checks</dfn>: steps that, given a [=trust anchor=], return whether it may be added to the [=local trust store=]. Local HTTPS uses one to keep at most one key per host.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>add a trust anchor</dfn> given a [=trust anchor/key=] |key|, a [=trust anchor/scope=] |scope| and, for [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=], a subject |subject|:

1. Let |anchor| be a [=trust anchor=] with |key|, |scope| and, if given, |subject|.
1. If the [=local trust store=] [=list/contains=] a [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] [=trust anchor=] with |key|, or the same [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] [=trust anchor=], return success.
1. If any [=trust anchor check=] returns false given |anchor|, return failure.
1. If |scope| is [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=], [=list/remove=] from the [=local trust store=] the [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] [=trust anchors=] with |key|, which it covers.
1. [=set/Append=] |anchor| to the [=local trust store=] and return success.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>remove a trust anchor</dfn> |anchor|:

1. [=set/Remove=] |anchor| from the [=local trust store=].
1. [=map/For each=] |identity| → |peer| of the [=known peers map=], if |peer| is no longer [=/authenticated=]:
    1. [=map/For each=] |origin| → |identities| of the [=peer grants=], if |identities| [=set/contains=] |identity|, [=revoke a peer grant=] given |origin| and |identity|.
    1. [=Close the sessions with a peer=] given |identity|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>replace a trust anchor key</dfn> |old| with |new|:

1. For each [=trust anchor=] in the [=local trust store=] whose [=trust anchor/key=] is |old|, set its [=trust anchor/key=] to |new|.
1. For each [=known peer=] whose [=known peer/anchor key=] is |old|, set it to |new|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>add a known peer</dfn> |peer|, which is on an [=protocol/authenticated=] session:

1. Set the [=known peers map=][|peer|'s [=peer/peer identity=]] to |peer|, recording as its [=known peer/anchor key=] and [=known peer/subject=] the ones it chains to on that session.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>forget a known peer</dfn> given a [=peer/peer identity=] |identity|:

1. [=map/For each=] |origin| → |identities| of the [=peer grants=], if |identities| [=set/contains=] |identity|, [=revoke a peer grant=] given |origin| and |identity|.
1. [=Close the sessions with a peer=] given |identity|.
1. [=map/Remove=] the [=known peers map=][|identity|].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>grant a peer</dfn> given an [=/origin=] |origin| and a [=peer/peer identity=] |identity|:

1. If the [=peer grants=][|origin|] does not [=map/exist=], set it to an empty [=/set=].
1. [=set/Append=] |identity| to the [=peer grants=][|origin|].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>revoke a peer grant</dfn> given an [=/origin=] |origin| and a [=peer/peer identity=] |identity|:

1. [=set/Remove=] |identity| from the [=peer grants=][|origin|], and [=map/remove=] the [=peer grants=][|origin|] if it is then empty.
1. [=Close the sessions with a peer=] given |identity| and |origin|.

</div>

When an origin's site data or permissions are cleared, the user agent runs [=revoke a peer grant=] given that origin and each [=peer/peer identity=] in its [=peer grants=].

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>close the sessions with a peer</dfn> given a [=peer/peer identity=] |identity| and an optional [=/origin=] |origin|:

1. For each {{LP2PConnection}} |connection| whose {{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} is with the peer with |identity|, and, if |origin| is given, whose [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=] is |origin|: [=close a session|close=] |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}}, and [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |connection|'s [=relevant global object=] to run [=LP2PConnection/finalize=] given |connection|, which fires {{LP2PConnection/close}}.

</div>

Note: [=Peer grants=] are origin-level while the [=local trust store=] is shared by all origins, so that the user doesn't need to authenticate the same peer again for every origin. Origins are kept apart by the [=peer grants=] and by the [=protocol/requesting origin=] of each session. [=Peer grants=] could be stored as the origin's permission data in the [=permission store=], as [Web Bluetooth](https://bluetooth.spec.whatwg.org/) does for its allowed devices.

Issue: Decide whether to back [=peer grants=] with the [=permission store=], as the extra permission data of a "local-peer-to-peer" powerful feature, which would also give a permission query and revocation.

Issue(WICG/local-peer-to-peer#24): Validate that a [=local trust store=] shared by all origins is appropriate, or whether [=trust anchors=] should be partitioned, for example per top-level site.

The user agent may persist its [=local trust store=], [=known peers=] and/or [=peer grants=]. If the user agent chooses to do so, it must do so in accordance with the persistent state rules of the [=transport binding=] in use. Such state is said to be <dfn>persisted</dfn>. Results of [=discover peers=], such as addresses, are not part of this state. The user agent should let the user view [=trust anchors=], [=known peers=] and [=peer grants=], and remove them with [=remove a trust anchor=], [=forget a known peer=] and [=revoke a peer grant=].

Peer advertisement {#peer-advertisement}
----------------------------------------

The user agent can make itself discoverable by advertising, using [=start advertising=].

Note: The user agent must get the user's explicit consent in order to start advertising.

When advertising, the user agent must listen for [=incoming sessions=]. Once an [=incoming session=] is [=protocol/authenticated=], the user agent runs [=add a known peer=] with the peer. The user agent must not directly provide access to an [=incoming session=] to any origin. Instead, unless the peer is already in the [=peer grants=] of the origin that initiated peer advertisement, the user agent must prompt to [grant peer](#peer-grant) access to that origin. Accepting the [=incoming session=] runs [=grant a peer=] for that origin, so later sessions from the peer do not prompt again. See [[#lp2p-receiver-incoming-session]].

The user agent must deliver an [=incoming session=] only to a started {{LP2PReceiver}} that <dfn lt="allows an origin">allows</dfn> the session's [=protocol/requesting origin=], and [=refuse=] the session if there is none. An {{LP2PReceiver}} [=allows an origin=] |origin| if |origin| is not an [=opaque origin=], and is [=same origin=] with the receiver's [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=] or with an [=list/item=] of its {{LP2PReceiver/[[AllowedOrigins]]}}.

Peer discovery {#peer-discovery}
--------------------------------

When asked to <dfn>start local peer discovery</dfn>, the user agent runs [=discover peers=].

Discovered peers are only shown in the picker, see [=acquire a local peer grant=]; they are not [=known peers=], whose entries are added or updated only from [=protocol/authenticated=] sessions, by [=add a known peer=]. The user agent must never expose the full result of peer discovery with an origin. Instead, the user agent must [=acquire a local peer grant=] to grant access to a peer for the origin that initiated the peer request.

Peer authentication {#peer-authentication}
------------------------------------------

A <dfn>trust anchor</dfn> is a key that the user agent trusts to authenticate [=peers=]. It has:

: <dfn for="trust anchor">key</dfn>
:: The key that peers' credentials chain to. What the credentials are, and how they chain to a key, is defined by the [=transport binding=].
: <dfn for="trust anchor">scope</dfn>
:: Either <dfn for="trust anchor/scope">one peer</dfn>, a single peer that the anchor vouches for, or <dfn for="trust anchor/scope">vouching</dfn>, any peer the anchor vouches for, within limits defined by the [=transport binding=].

Adding a [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] [=trust anchor=] removes the [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] [=trust anchors=] with the same [=trust anchor/key=], which it covers; see [=add a trust anchor=].

A [=trust anchor=] is added to the [=local trust store=] only by a <dfn>trust ceremony</dfn>: a run of [=authenticate a session=] in which the user takes part, for example by entering a PIN or scanning a QR code. A [=trust ceremony=] with a peer runs [=add a trust anchor=] scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=]: that peer. If the peer is a party that vouches for others, as the [=transport binding=] defines, and the user chooses to trust it for its devices, the ceremony instead runs [=add a trust anchor=] with the peer's key scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=]. So a [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=] [=trust anchor=] is only added by a [=trust ceremony=] with the vouching party itself. If [=add a trust anchor=] returns failure, the ceremony fails.

A [=peer=] is said to be <dfn>authenticated</dfn> when its credentials chain to the [=trust anchor/key=] of a [=trust anchor=] in the [=local trust store=], and the peer is within that anchor's [=trust anchor/scope=].

A peer's credentials may change over time without changing its [=peer/peer identity=], as long as they chain to the same [=trust anchor/key=]. Whether a [=trust anchor/key=] can be replaced with continuity is defined by the [=transport binding=]; if so, the user agent runs [=replace a trust anchor key=]. Otherwise, a new key needs a new [=trust ceremony=].

Issue: Define how a peer can move to another [=trust anchor=]'s [=trust anchor/key=] while keeping its [=peer/peer identity=] and [=peer grants=], for example by proving that it holds both keys.

<div class="example" id="example-trust-anchors">
    * A device paired directly that uses its own key, which makes it <dfn>self-anchored</dfn>: its key, scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=], that device.
    * A device paired directly whose credentials are issued by a hub: the hub's key, scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=], that device. The device's credentials can rotate freely, and other devices of the hub are not trusted.
    * A hub the user chose to trust for its devices, through a [=trust ceremony=] with the hub: the hub's key, scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/vouching=]. It replaces the [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=] anchors for that hub's devices, which keep the same [=peer/peer identity=]. Removing it removes trust in all of the hub's devices.
</div>

Note: [=Trust anchors=] may also be used to validate HTTPS servers on the [=local communication medium=], each constrained to a namespace of local names; see the separate [Local HTTPS](https://wicg.github.io/local-peer-to-peer/local-https.html) draft.

Peer grant {#peer-grant}
------------------------------------

The user agent, with the user's consent, must [=acquire a local peer grant=] for an origin to get access to a peer.

Note: The user agent must get the user's explicit consent in order to grant an origin access to a peer. For enhanced privacy protection, the user agent must provide means to dismiss any related user interface and this action must not be detectable by script or have script-observable side-effects. The user interface may provide means to revoke the grant or to make the grant [=persisted=].

When asked to <dfn>acquire a local peer grant</dfn> for an origin, the user agent displays the peers that [=discover peers=] reports to the user through its native user interface, marking those that are [=known peers=], and returns the peer the user selects, or null if the user dismisses the user interface. The user agent may leave out peers whose [=peer/capabilities=] lack the features the page needs. Selecting a peer is the user's consent. Once a session with the selected peer is [=protocol/authenticated=] and [=accepted=], the user agent runs [=grant a peer=] for the origin. See {{LP2PRequest/start()}}.

Transport abstraction {#transport-abstraction}
==============================================

The API and its algorithms are defined against the abstract concepts and operations in this section. A <dfn>transport binding</dfn> defines how a concrete protocol implements them. This specification defines one [=transport binding=], in [[#osp-binding]]. Which bindings a user agent implements is [=implementation-defined=]. A [=peer=] is associated with the binding through which it was discovered or through which its session arrived, and all operations involving that peer use that binding.

A [=transport binding=] must provide end-to-end confidentiality and integrity for all communication with a peer, including when the path to the peer goes through an intermediary.

Peers and sessions {#transport-concepts}
----------------------------------------

A [=peer=] has the following, provided by its [=transport binding=]. None of them is exposed to an origin.

: <dfn for="peer">peer identity</dfn>
:: An opaque value that identifies the peer, stable across sessions, scoped to the [=transport binding=]. It is derived from the [=trust anchor/key=] that the peer's credentials chain to and the peer's subject under it; for a [=self-anchored=] peer, the subject is its own key. It is only trusted once the peer is [=/authenticated=]. The [=known peers map=] and [=peer grants=] are keyed on it.
: <dfn for="peer">display name</dfn>
:: A name for the peer device, for display in user agent user interface, or null. It is asserted by the peer's user agent, not by script. Trusted only if obtained on a session that is or becomes [=protocol/authenticated=], or by other means the user agent considers trustworthy; otherwise, such as during [=discover peers=], it is a display hint.
: <dfn for="peer">nickname</dfn>
:: The nickname given by the remote page when it started advertising, or null. It is chosen by script and never verified.
: <dfn for="peer">capabilities</dfn>
:: The [=/set=] of features the peer supports: <dfn for="peer/capabilities">data channels</dfn> and <dfn for="peer/capabilities">WebTransport</dfn>. Like the [=peer/display name=], it is asserted by the peer's user agent and is only a hint until the peer is [=/authenticated=].

A user agent [=refuses=] an [=incoming data channel=] or [=incoming WebTransport session=] for a feature it has not enabled.

A <dfn for="protocol">Local Peer-to-Peer session</dfn> is a confidential connection with one [=peer=]. It is <dfn for="protocol">authenticated</dfn> if the credentials the peer presented in it chain to a [=trust anchor=] in the [=local trust store=], which verifies the [=peer/peer identity=] of the peer for it.

A [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] that is exposed to an origin is not shared with other origins. A binding may carry several sessions with the same peer over one underlying connection, or use a separate connection for each.

For each [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], the side that established it with [=establish a session=] is the <dfn for="protocol">client</dfn>, and the side that reported it as an [=incoming session=] is the <dfn for="protocol">server</dfn>. Once established, the session is symmetric: either side can use any operation on it, except that:

* the [=protocol/server=] validates the [=protocol/requesting origin=];
* where the binding needs it to avoid collisions, identifiers such as [=data channel/id=]s and streams follow the role;
* only the [=protocol/client=] opens any additional underlying connection for the session.

A [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] that is exposed to an origin has a <dfn for="protocol">requesting origin</dfn>: the [=/origin=] on whose behalf the [=protocol/client=] established it. The [=protocol/client=] conveys it inside the session, and the [=transport binding=] binds it to the [=protocol/authenticated=] session, for example by carrying it on the channel that authentication covers. The [=protocol/server=] checks it as described in [[#peer-advertisement]].

The [=protocol/requesting origin=] reveals which sites the user is using, and the origins that a [=protocol/server=] accepts reveal which sites its user is using. Neither is exposed to a peer that is not [=/authenticated=]. A [=transport binding=] may let the [=protocol/server=] find out earlier whether it would accept the [=protocol/requesting origin=], so that a [=trust ceremony=] is not wasted on a session it [=refuses=], only with a mechanism that exposes neither. Otherwise, a first [=trust ceremony=] with a peer can end in a refusal.

Issue: Follow up on REQ-NAME-05, "Privacy-Preserving Discovery", of the IETF SETTLE [requirements](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-sweet-settle-requirements/), for a mechanism that lets a [=protocol/server=] decide earlier without exposing either side's origins.

The [=protocol/requesting origin=] is asserted by the peer's user agent. The binding guarantees that it is consistent for the session, not that it is authentic: it does not prove that the remote page was served by that origin. User agents must not grant anything that belongs to the [=protocol/requesting origin=], such as its permissions, storage or credentials, on the strength of it. This is the same model as the `Origin` header in [[FETCH]].

Device keys, credentials and anything else that would let an origin authenticate as the user agent must not be exposed to origins. Instead, each [=WebTransport session=] has its own keying material exporter, see [=export keying material=], so that applications can prove their origins with credentials their own origin issued.

A <dfn>data channel</dfn> is a reliable, ordered, message-preserving, bidirectional channel on a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]. Either side can open one. It has an <dfn for="data channel">id</dfn>, an integer between 0 and 65,534 that is unique within the session, a <dfn for="data channel">label</dfn> and a <dfn for="data channel">protocol</dfn>. A message on a [=data channel=] is a sequence of bytes, marked as either text or binary.

Operations {#transport-operations}
----------------------------------

A [=transport binding=] provides the following operations. Any of them can fail, including because the peer does not support it.

: To <dfn>start advertising</dfn> given a nickname
:: Make the user agent discoverable through [=discover peers=] on other user agents, with the given [=peer/nickname=] if the binding can carry it, and accept sessions from peers. This returns an <dfn>advertisement</dfn>. Each [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] a peer establishes with it is reported as an <dfn>incoming session</dfn> for that [=advertisement=]. To <dfn>stop advertising</dfn> given an [=advertisement=] ends both.
: To <dfn>discover peers</dfn>
:: Report the [=peers=] advertising on the [=local communication medium=], each with its [=peer/display name=] and [=peer/capabilities=] as far as the binding knows them, as they appear and disappear, until asked to <dfn>stop discovering peers</dfn>. A discovered [=peer/peer identity=] is not verified.
: To <dfn>establish a session</dfn> with a [=peer=] given an [=/origin=]
:: Establish a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] with the peer, with that origin as its [=protocol/requesting origin=]. On the peer, the session is reported as an [=incoming session=], which the peer's user agent later <dfn lt="accept|accepted">accepts</dfn> or <dfn lt="refuse|refused">refuses</dfn>. The [=protocol/client=] is notified of either. Refusing closes the session.
: To <dfn>authenticate a session</dfn>
:: Mutually authenticate the two sides of a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] and verify the [=peer/peer identity=] of the peer, so that the session is [=protocol/authenticated=]. If the peer's credentials do not chain to a [=trust anchor=] in the [=local trust store=], this is a [=trust ceremony=], which requires user interaction on one or both devices and runs [=add a trust anchor=] as described in [[#peer-authentication]]. Only the [=protocol/client=] initiates it; the [=protocol/server=] only responds.
: To <dfn>open a data channel</dfn> on an [=protocol/authenticated=] [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] given a label, a protocol and an optional id
:: Open a [=data channel=] with that [=data channel/label=] and [=data channel/protocol=], and with the given [=data channel/id=] or, if none is given, one chosen by the binding. The other side is notified of an <dfn>incoming data channel</dfn>, which its user agent accepts or refuses. The operation succeeds if it is accepted.
: To <dfn>send data on a channel</dfn> given a [=data channel=] and a message
:: Queue the message for delivery on the [=data channel=]. The other side is notified of a <dfn>received message</dfn>.
: To <dfn>close a data channel</dfn>
:: Close the [=data channel=] in both directions. The other side is notified that it was closed.
: To <dfn>close a session</dfn>
:: Close the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], all of its [=data channels=] and, abruptly, all of its [=WebTransport sessions=]. The other side is notified that it was closed. The binding also reports a session as closed when the underlying transport fails.
: To <dfn>open a WebTransport session</dfn> over an [=protocol/authenticated=] [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]
:: Establish a session with the same peer that provides the capabilities of a WebTransport session as defined in [[!webtransport]], bound to the same [=peer/peer identity=]. The other side is notified of an <dfn>incoming WebTransport session</dfn>, which its user agent accepts or refuses. The operation succeeds if it is accepted. The session either delivers datagrams unreliably ("`supports-unreliable`") or, if the binding cannot lose messages, reliably ("`reliable-only`"), as WebTransport over HTTP/2 does.
: To <dfn>close a WebTransport session</dfn> given a close code and a reason
:: Close the [=WebTransport session=] and its streams. The other side is notified that it was closed, with that code and reason. The [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] stays open. The binding also reports a [=WebTransport session=] as closed abruptly, without a code and reason, when it fails or its [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] closes.
: To <dfn>export keying material</dfn> from a [=WebTransport session=] given a label, a context and a length
:: Return keying material of that length that both sides of the [=WebTransport session=] derive identically and that no other [=WebTransport session=] yields, including one carried over the same underlying connection. The binding derives it as [WebTransport over HTTP/3, Section 4.8](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-webtrans-http3#section-4.8) does: a TLS exporter [[!RFC9846]] whose context holds an identifier of the [=WebTransport session=], the label and the context. The [=protocol/requesting origin=] need not be part of it, since a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] is not shared between origins.
: To <dfn>drain a WebTransport session</dfn>
:: Ask the other side to finish using the [=WebTransport session=] and close it. Both sides can still use it.

Note: [=Data channels=] ({{LP2PDataChannel}}) and WebTransport sessions ({{LP2PQuicTransport}}) are two independent ways to communicate over a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]; neither is built on the other.

Issue(29): Decide whether to keep both data channels and WebTransport sessions, or converge on one.

<section algorithm="LP2PReceiver">

LP2PReceiver Interface {#lp2p-receiver}
================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PReceiver</dfn> interface allows advertising on the [=local communication medium=], enabling other peers to discover and connect.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PReceiver : EventTarget {
  constructor(optional LP2PReceiverOptions options = {});
  attribute EventHandler onconnection;
  Promise&lt;undefined&gt; start();
  undefined stop();
};
</pre>

An {{LP2PReceiver}} has the following internal slots:

: <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[State]]</dfn>
:: "`initial`", "`advertising`" or "`stopped`", initially "`initial`".
: <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[StartPromise]]</dfn>
:: A {{Promise}} or null, initially null.
: <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[Advertisement]]</dfn>
:: An [=advertisement=] or null, initially null.
: <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[Nickname]]</dfn>
:: A string.
: <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[AllowedOrigins]]</dfn>
:: A [=/list=] of [=/origins=].
: <dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver">\[[ConnectionSteps]]</dfn>
:: Steps that take an {{LP2PConnection}}, run in place of the {{LP2PReceiver/connection}} event, or null.

An {{LP2PReceiver}} is used once: after {{LP2PReceiver/stop()}} it cannot be started again.

<div algorithm="LP2PReceiver constructor">
The <dfn constructor for="LP2PReceiver">LP2PReceiver(|options|)</dfn> constructor steps are to [=set up an LP2PReceiver=] [=this=] given |options|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>set up an LP2PReceiver</dfn> |receiver| given |options| and optional |connectionSteps|:

1. Set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[ConnectionSteps]]}} to |connectionSteps| if given, or null otherwise.
1. Set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[Nickname]]}} to |options|["{{LP2PReceiverOptions/nickname}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or the empty string otherwise.
1. Set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[AllowedOrigins]]}} to an empty [=/list=].
1. [=list/For each=] |entry| of |options|["{{LP2PReceiverOptions/allowedOrigins}}"]:
    1. Let |url| be the result of running the [=URL parser=] on |entry|.
    1. If |url| is failure, throw a {{TypeError}}.
    1. [=list/Append=] |url|'s [=url/origin=] to |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[AllowedOrigins]]}}. An [=opaque origin=] appended here never matches, see [=allows an origin=].

</div>

<div algorithm="LP2PReceiver start">
The <dfn method for="LP2PReceiver">start()</dfn> method steps are to return the result of [=start an LP2PReceiver|starting=] [=this=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>start an LP2PReceiver</dfn> |receiver|:

1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`stopped`", return [=a promise rejected with=] an "{{InvalidStateError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} is not null, return it.
1. Let |global| be |receiver|'s [=relevant global object=].
1. If |global| is not permitted to use the "local-peer-to-peer" [=policy-controlled feature=], as described in [[#permission-policy-integration]], or |receiver|'s [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=] is an [=opaque origin=], return [=a promise rejected with=] a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Let |promise| be [=a new promise=].
1. Set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} to |promise|.
1. Run these steps [=in parallel=]:
    1. Ask the user for permission to advertise |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[Nickname]]}} on the [=local communication medium=] on behalf of |receiver|'s [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=], as described in [[#peer-advertisement]].
    1. If the user did not grant permission, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} to null and [=reject=] |promise| with a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}, and abort these steps.
    1. Let |advertisement| be the result of [=start advertising=] given |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[Nickname]]}}. If this fails, [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} to null and [=reject=] |promise| with a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}, and abort these steps.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to run these steps:
        1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`stopped`", [=stop advertising=] given |advertisement| and abort these steps.
        1. Set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[Advertisement]]}} to |advertisement|.
        1. Set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} to "`advertising`".
        1. [=Resolve=] |promise| with undefined.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

Note: Whether the user declined to advertise or dismissed the user interface, the user agent takes the same steps, so that script cannot tell the two apart. See [[#peer-grant]].

<div algorithm="LP2PReceiver stop">
The <dfn method for="LP2PReceiver">stop()</dfn> method steps are to [=stop an LP2PReceiver|stop=] [=this=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>stop an LP2PReceiver</dfn> |receiver|:

1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`stopped`", return.
1. Set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} to "`stopped`".
1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[StartPromise]]}} is not null and is pending, [=reject=] it with an "{{AbortError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[Advertisement]]}} is not null, [=stop advertising=] given it and set |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[Advertisement]]}} to null.

</div>

Stopping does not affect the {{LP2PConnection}} objects already delivered; their [=Local Peer-to-Peer sessions=] stay open until closed.

Incoming sessions {#lp2p-receiver-incoming-session}
---------------------------------------------------

<div algorithm="LP2PReceiver incoming session">
When an [=incoming session=] |session| is reported, the user agent must run these steps [=in parallel=]:

1. If |session| is not [=protocol/authenticated=], wait for the [=protocol/client=] to run [=authenticate a session=] on it, and respond. This may be a [=trust ceremony=]. If it fails, [=close a session|close=] |session| and abort these steps.
1. Let |receiver| be the {{LP2PReceiver}} whose {{LP2PReceiver/[[Advertisement]]}} is the [=advertisement=] |session| was reported for, that is, the one the [=protocol/client=] picked, if its {{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is "`advertising`" and it [=allows an origin|allows=] the [=protocol/requesting origin=]. Otherwise, let |receiver| be another {{LP2PReceiver}} for which this holds, chosen by the user agent, for example by asking the user. If there is none, [=refuse=] |session| and abort these steps.
1. Let |origin| be |receiver|'s [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=].
1. [=Add a known peer=] with the peer.
1. If the peer's [=peer/peer identity=] is not in the [=peer grants=] of |origin|, ask the user to grant the peer access to |origin|. If the user declines or dismisses the prompt, [=refuse=] |session| and abort these steps. Otherwise, [=grant a peer=] given |origin| and the [=peer/peer identity=].
1. [=Accept=] |session|.
1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |receiver|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:
    1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[State]]}} is not "`advertising`", [=close a session|close=] |session| and abort these steps.
    1. Let |connection| be the result of [=creating an LP2PConnection=] given |session| and |receiver|'s [=relevant global object=].
    1. If |receiver|.{{LP2PReceiver/[[ConnectionSteps]]}} is not null, run them given |connection| and abort these steps.
    1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PReceiver/connection}} at |receiver| using {{LP2PConnectionEvent}} with its {{LP2PConnectionEvent/connection}} attribute initialized to |connection|.

</div>

LP2PReceiverOptions {#lp2p-receiver-options}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PReceiverOptions {
    DOMString nickname;
    sequence&lt;USVString&gt; allowedOrigins = [];
};
</pre>

: <dfn dict-member for="LP2PReceiverOptions">allowedOrigins</dfn>
:: A list of URLs whose [=url/origins=], in addition to the receiver's own origin, the receiver [=allows an origin|allows=] as the [=protocol/requesting origin=] of an [=incoming session=]. By default, only same-origin sessions are delivered. An entry that fails to parse makes the constructor throw a {{TypeError}}. An entry whose origin is an [=opaque origin=], such as a `data:` URL, never matches.

Issue: Decide the granularity of {{LP2PReceiverOptions/allowedOrigins}}, the [=protocol/requesting origin=] and [=peer grants=]: per origin or per site, and whether to allow wildcards or any origin.

LP2PConnectionEvent {#lp2p-receiver-connection-event}
----------------------------------------------------

Issue: In general, when defining a new interface that inherits from Event please always ask feedback from the WHATWG or the W3C WebApps WG community. See [defining event interfaces](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#defining-event-interfaces).

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PConnectionEvent : Event {
    constructor(DOMString type, LP2PConnectionEventInit connectionEventInitDict);
    readonly attribute LP2PConnection connection;
};

dictionary LP2PConnectionEventInit : EventInit {
    required LP2PConnection connection;
};
</pre>

Events {#lp2p-receiver-events}
------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PReceiver}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PReceiver"><code>connection</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PConnectionEvent}}</td>
      <td>An [=incoming session=] was accepted; it is exposed as an {{LP2PConnection}}.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Event handlers {#lp2p-receiver-event-handlers}
----------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the [[#lp2p-receiver|LP2PReceiver]] interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PReceiver"><code>onconnection</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PReceiver/connection}}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Examples {#lp2p-receiver-examples}
---------------------------------

Example: Setting up a receiver to listen for connections:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
    // Also accept sessions requested by this origin.
    allowedOrigins: ["https://partner.example"],
});
receiver.onconnection = e => {
    console.log("Connection established!");
    const conn = e.connection;
    conn.onclose = () => console.log("Connection closed.");
};

// Blocks until permission is received.
await receiver.start();

// Later: stop advertising. Delivered connections stay open.
receiver.stop();
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PRequest">

The LP2PRequest Interface {#lp2p-request}
================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PRequest</dfn> interface represents a request for a connection to another local peer.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PRequest {
    constructor(optional LP2PRequestOptions options = {});
    Promise&lt;LP2PConnection&gt; start(optional LP2PRequestStartOptions options = {});
};
</pre>

An {{LP2PRequest}} has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PRequest">\[[StartPromise]]</dfn>, a {{Promise}} or null, initially null, and a <dfn attribute for="LP2PRequest">\[[Nickname]]</dfn>, a string.

<div algorithm="LP2PRequest constructor">
The <dfn constructor for="LP2PRequest">LP2PRequest(|options|)</dfn> constructor steps are:

1. Set [=this=].{{LP2PRequest/[[Nickname]]}} to |options|["{{LP2PRequestOptions/nickname}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or the empty string otherwise.

</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="LP2PRequest">start(|options|)</dfn> method steps are to return the result of [=start an LP2PRequest|starting=] [=this=] given |options|["{{LP2PRequestStartOptions/signal}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or null otherwise.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>start an LP2PRequest</dfn> |request| given an {{AbortSignal}} or null |signal|:

1. If |signal| is not null and is [=AbortSignal/aborted=], return [=a promise rejected with=] |signal|'s [=AbortSignal/abort reason=].
1. If |request|.{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} is not null, return it.
1. Let |global| be |request|'s [=relevant global object=].
1. Let |origin| be |request|'s [=relevant settings object=]'s [=environment settings object/origin=].
1. If |global| is not permitted to use the "local-peer-to-peer" [=policy-controlled feature=], as described in [[#permission-policy-integration]], or |origin| is an [=opaque origin=], return [=a promise rejected with=] a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Let |promise| be [=a new promise=].
1. Set |request|.{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} to |promise|.
1. Let |aborted| be false.
1. Let |fail| be these steps: [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to run these steps: if |aborted| is false, set |request|.{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} to null and [=reject=] |promise| with a "{{NotAllowedError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. If |signal| is not null, [=AbortSignal/add=] the following abort steps to |signal|:
    1. If |promise| is settled, abort these steps.
    1. Set |aborted| to true.
    1. Set |request|.{{LP2PRequest/[[StartPromise]]}} to null.
    1. [=Reject=] |promise| with |signal|'s [=AbortSignal/abort reason=].
1. Run these steps [=in parallel=]. Whenever |aborted| becomes true, take down any user interface they show, [=stop discovering peers=], [=close a session|close=] |session| if it was established, and abort these steps:
    1. [=Start local peer discovery=].
    1. Let |peer| be the result of [=acquire a local peer grant=] for |origin|, presenting |request|.{{LP2PRequest/[[Nickname]]}} to the user.
    1. [=Stop discovering peers=].
    1. If |peer| is null, run |fail| and abort these steps.
    1. Let |session| be the result of [=establish a session=] with |peer| given |origin|. If this fails, run |fail| and abort these steps.
    1. Wait until |session| is [=protocol/authenticated=] and the [=protocol/server=] has [=accepted=] it. Meanwhile, run [=authenticate a session=] on |session| when the [=transport binding=] calls for it. This may be a [=trust ceremony=]. If authentication fails, the [=protocol/server=] [=refuses=] |session|, or |session| is closed, [=close a session|close=] |session|, run |fail| and abort these steps.
    1. [=Add a known peer=] with |peer|, and [=grant a peer=] given |origin| and |peer|'s [=peer/peer identity=].
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |global| to run these steps:
        1. If |aborted| is true, [=close a session|close=] |session| and abort these steps.
        1. Let |connection| be the result of [=creating an LP2PConnection=] given |session| and |global|.
        1. [=Resolve=] |promise| with |connection|.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

Calling {{LP2PRequest/start()}} again while the request is pending, or after it succeeded, returns the same promise, and ignores the new options. Aborting after it succeeded does nothing; close the connection instead. After a rejection, {{LP2PRequest/start()}} can be called again to start over.

Every failure rejects with the same error, so that script cannot tell whether the user dismissed the picker, or picked a peer that then could not be reached, could not be [=/authenticated=] or [=refused=] the session. Refusal after the user picked a peer is expected: discovery does not carry the [=protocol/requesting origin=], so the picker can list peers whose receivers do not [=allows an origin|allow=] it.

LP2PRequestOptions {#lp2p-request-options}
---------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PRequestOptions {
    DOMString nickname;
};

dictionary LP2PRequestStartOptions {
    AbortSignal signal;
};
</pre>

: <dfn dict-member for="LP2PRequestStartOptions">signal</dfn>
:: Aborts {{LP2PRequest/start()}}, as {{RequestInit/signal}} aborts {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/fetch()}}: the promise rejects with the signal's [=AbortSignal/abort reason=], and the picker or the session being set up is taken down.

Examples {#lp2p-request-examples}
---------------------------------

Example: Setting up a request for a connection:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

try {
    // Blocks until the user picked a peer and it accepted the session.
    const conn = await request.start();
    console.log("Connection established!");

    // Later: close the connection, its data channels and its transports.
    conn.close();
} catch (e) {
    // NotAllowedError: dismissed, refused or failed; script can't tell which.
}
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PConnection">

The LP2PConnection Interface {#lp2p-connection}
===============================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PConnection</dfn> interface represents a connection with another local peer.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PConnection : EventTarget {
  undefined close();
  attribute EventHandler onclose;
};
</pre>

An {{LP2PConnection}} has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection">\[[Session]]</dfn>, a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]; a <dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection">\[[Transports]]</dfn>, a [=/set=] of {{LP2PQuicTransport}} objects, initially empty; and a <dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection">\[[Closed]]</dfn>, a boolean, initially false.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>create an LP2PConnection</dfn> given a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] |session| and a [=global object=] |global|:

1. Let |connection| be a [=new=] {{LP2PConnection}} in |global|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. Set |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} to |session|.
1. Return |connection|.

</div>

<div algorithm="LP2PConnection close">
The <dfn method for="LP2PConnection">close()</dfn> method steps are to [=close an LP2PConnection|close=] [=this=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>close an LP2PConnection</dfn> |connection|:

1. If |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, return.
1. [=Close a session|Close=] |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}}.
1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |connection|'s [=relevant global object=] to run [=LP2PConnection/finalize=] given |connection|.

</div>

When the [=transport binding=] reports that the {{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} of an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection| was closed, by the peer or because the underlying transport failed, the user agent must [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |connection|'s [=relevant global object=] to run [=LP2PConnection/finalize=] given |connection|.

<div algorithm>
To <dfn for="LP2PConnection">finalize</dfn> an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection|:

1. If |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, return.
1. Set |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} to true.
1. [=set/For each=] |transport| of |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Transports]]}}, fail |transport| abruptly with a {{WebTransportError}} whose `source` is "`session`", as {{WebTransport}} does when its session is terminated without a close code, unless it is already closed or failed.
1. If |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Listener]]}} is not null, [=close an LP2PQuicTransportListener|close=] it.
1. [=Fire an event=] named {{LP2PConnection/close}} at |connection|.

</div>

Issue: Decide whether {{LP2PConnection}} also gets a keying material exporter, for pages that only use [=data channels=]. It would need a per-connection context, since several connections with the same peer can share the underlying transport.

The {{LP2PConnection/close}} event fires at most once. Closing the session also closes all of its [=data channels=].

Events {#lp2p-connection-events}
--------------------------------

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PConnection"><code>close</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The connection is closed, by {{LP2PConnection/close()}}, by the peer, or because the transport failed.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

The <dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection"><code>onclose</code></dfn> [=event handler IDL attribute=] has the [=event handler event type=] {{LP2PConnection/close}}.

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PDataChannel">

The LP2PDataChannel Interface {#lp2p-data-channel}
==========================================

Issue: This section is not yet aligned with the rest of this specification, nor fully specified.

The LP2PDataChannel interface represents a bi-directional data channel between two peers. An LP2PDataChannel is created via a [factory method](#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions) on a LP2PConnection object. It is backed by a [=data channel=] on the connection's [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=].

Note: The LP2PDataChannel interface is purposefully kept as close as possible to the [RTCDataChannel](https://www.w3.org/TR/webrtc/#rtcdatachannel) interface defined in [[!webrtc]]. The aim is to allow seamless transition of developers that are familiar with WebRTC as well as allowing libraries to easily work with both the [[!webrtc]] and LP2P API.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PDataChannel : EventTarget {
  constructor(USVString label,
              optional LP2PDataChannelInit dataChannelDict = {});
  readonly attribute USVString label;
  readonly attribute USVString protocol;
  readonly attribute unsigned short? id;
  attribute EventHandler onopen;
  attribute EventHandler onerror;
  attribute EventHandler onclosing;
  attribute EventHandler onclose;
  undefined close();
  attribute EventHandler onmessage;
  attribute BinaryType binaryType;
  undefined send((USVString or Blob or ArrayBuffer or ArrayBufferView) data);
};
</pre>

LP2PDataChannelInit {#lp2p-data-channel-init}
----------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
dictionary LP2PDataChannelInit {
  USVString protocol = "";
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short id;
};
</pre>

Events {#lp2p-data-channel-events}
---------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PDataChannel}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>open</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The data channel is opened.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>message</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{MessageEvent}}[[html]]</td>
      <td>An incoming message is received.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>error</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>An error occurred.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>closing</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The data channel is closing.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>close</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{Event}}</td>
      <td>The data channel is closed.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Event handlers {#lp2p-data-channel-event-handlers}
---------------------------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the [[#lp2p-data-channel|LP2PDataChannel]] interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onopen</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/open}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onmessage</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/message}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onerror</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/error}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onclosing</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/closing}}</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PDataChannel"><code>onclose</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannel/close}}</td>
    </tr>
</table>

LP2PConnection Interface Extensions {#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
partial interface LP2PConnection {
  LP2PDataChannel createDataChannel(USVString label,
                                    optional LP2PDataChannelInit dataChannelDict = {});
  attribute EventHandler ondatachannel;
};
</pre>

{{LP2PConnection/createDataChannel()}} throws a "{{NotSupportedError}}" {{DOMException}} if the [=peer/capabilities=] of the connection's peer do not include [=peer/capabilities/data channels=].

LP2PDataChannelEvent {#lp2p-data-channel-event}
------------------------------------------------

Issue: In general, when defining a new interface that inherits from Event please always ask feedback from the WHATWG or the W3C WebApps WG community. See [defining event interfaces](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#defining-event-interfaces).

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PDataChannelEvent : Event {
  constructor(DOMString type, LP2PDataChannelEventInit dataChannelEventInitDict);
  readonly attribute LP2PDataChannel channel;
};

dictionary LP2PDataChannelEventInit : EventInit {
    required LP2PDataChannel channel;
};
</pre>

Extensions Events {#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions-events}
------------------------------

The following event is [=fire an event|fired=] at the {{LP2PConnection}} object:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Event name</th>
      <th>Interface</th>
      <th>Fired when&mldr;</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn event for="LP2PConnection"><code>datachannel</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PDataChannelEvent}}</td>
      <td>An incoming data channel is received.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

An [=incoming data channel=] that is reported before its {{LP2PConnection}} is exposed to script, that is, before {{LP2PRequest/start()}} resolves or the {{LP2PReceiver/connection}} event is dispatched, is held until then, so that script can set {{LP2PConnection/ondatachannel}} first. This is the same rule as for [=incoming WebTransport sessions=].

Extensions Event handlers {#lp2p-connection-data-channel-extensions-event-handlers}
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following are the <a>event handlers</a> (and their corresponding <a>event handler event types</a>) that must be supported, as <a>event handler IDL attributes</a>, by all objects implementing the {{LP2PConnection}} interface:

<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th><a>event handler</a></th>
      <th><a>event handler event type</a></th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection"><code>ondatachannel</code></dfn></td>
      <td>{{LP2PConnection/datachannel}}</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

Examples {#data-channel-communication-examples}
---------------------------------------------------

Example: Receive a {{LP2PDataChannel}} on an existing connection as receiver:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});

receiver.onconnection = e => {
    const conn = e.connection;
    console.log("Receiver: Got a connection!");

    conn.ondatachannel = e => {
        const channel = e.channel;

        channel.onmessage = e => {
            const message = e.data;
            console.log(\`Receiver: Received message: ${message}\`);
        };

        channel.send("Good day to you, requester!");
    };
};

await receiver.start();
</pre>

Example: Create a {{LP2PDataChannel}} on an existing connection as requester:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});

const conn = await request.start();
console.log("Requester: Got a connection!");

const channel = conn.createDataChannel("My Channel");

channel.onopen = e => {
    channel.onmessage = e => {
        const message = e.data;
        console.log(\`Requester: Received message: ${message}\`);
    };

    channel.send("Good day to you, receiver!");
};
</pre>

</section>

<section algorithm="LP2PQuicTransport">

The LP2PQuicTransport Interface {#lp2p-quic-transport}
======================================================

The <dfn interface>LP2PQuicTransport</dfn> interface is a [[!webtransport]] {{WebTransport}} over an {{LP2PConnection}}. It is backed by a <dfn>WebTransport session</dfn>: a session obtained with [=open a WebTransport session=] on the connection's {{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}}, or an [=incoming WebTransport session=] on it.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PQuicTransport : WebTransport {
  constructor(LP2PConnection connection,
              optional LP2PQuicTransportInit options = {});
  readonly attribute LP2PConnection connection;
};

dictionary LP2PQuicTransportInit {
  boolean requireUnreliable = false;
  WebTransportCongestionControl congestionControl = "default";
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short? anticipatedConcurrentIncomingUnidirectionalStreams = null;
  [EnforceRange] unsigned short? anticipatedConcurrentIncomingBidirectionalStreams = null;
};
</pre>

The members of {{LP2PQuicTransportInit}} have the meaning of the {{WebTransportOptions}} members of the same name. {{WebTransportOptions}} members that concern HTTP or a server, such as `allowPooling`, `serverCertificateHashes` and `protocols`, are left out.

: <dfn dict-member for="LP2PQuicTransportInit">requireUnreliable</dfn>
:: If true, the transport fails unless its [=WebTransport session=] delivers datagrams unreliably, as for an HTTP/2 fallback in [[!webtransport]]. A [=transport binding=] that cannot lose messages provides only "`reliable-only`" sessions; see [=open a WebTransport session=].

{{LP2PQuicTransport}} inherits the attributes and methods of {{WebTransport}}, but not its constructor. [[!webtransport]] defines the internal slots and algorithms of {{WebTransport}} in terms of a WebTransport session over HTTP/3 or HTTP/2. For an {{LP2PQuicTransport}} they apply to its [=WebTransport session=] instead: its streams, datagrams, close and draining are those of the [=transport binding=].

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>initialize an LP2PQuicTransport</dfn> |transport| given |options|, initialize the internal slots of |transport| as the {{WebTransport}} constructor does, given the {{WebTransportOptions}} members of |options|, except that there is no URL, no HTTP headers, no server certificate hashes and no pooling, and its `protocol` is the empty string.

</div>

Its `exportKeyingMaterial()` method runs [=export keying material=] on its [=WebTransport session=], in place of the exporter of WebTransport over HTTP/3. Each [=WebTransport session=] has its own exporter.

An {{LP2PQuicTransport}} also has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransport">\[[Connection]]</dfn>, an {{LP2PConnection}}. The <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransport">connection</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=].{{LP2PQuicTransport/[[Connection]]}}.

Issue: Ask the WebTransport editors for a hook to create a {{WebTransport}} over a session that is not HTTP, instead of initializing its internal slots here.

<div algorithm="LP2PQuicTransport constructor">
The <dfn constructor for="LP2PQuicTransport">LP2PQuicTransport(|connection|, |options|)</dfn> constructor steps are to [=set up an LP2PQuicTransport=] [=this=] given |connection| and |options|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>set up an LP2PQuicTransport</dfn> |transport| given an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection| and |options|:

1. If |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, throw an "{{InvalidStateError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. If the [=peer/capabilities=] of the peer of |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} do not include [=peer/capabilities/WebTransport=], throw a "{{NotSupportedError}}" {{DOMException}}. They are known when the connection exists, so this fails synchronously, as {{WebTransport}}'s constructor does for options it cannot support.
1. [=Initialize an LP2PQuicTransport=] |transport| given |options|. Its state is "`connecting`" and its `reliability` is "`pending`".
1. Set |transport|.{{LP2PQuicTransport/[[Connection]]}} to |connection| and [=set/append=] |transport| to |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Transports]]}}.
1. Run these steps [=in parallel=]:
    1. Let |session| be the result of [=open a WebTransport session=] on |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}}.
    1. [=Queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |transport|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:
        1. If opening failed, or the peer refused, fail |transport| with a {{WebTransportError}} whose `source` is "`session`", as {{WebTransport}} does when establishing a session fails, and abort these steps.
        1. If |transport|'s state is not "`connecting`", close |session| and abort these steps.
        1. If |options|["{{LP2PQuicTransportInit/requireUnreliable}}"] is true and |session| is "`reliable-only`", close |session|, fail |transport| as above, and abort these steps.
        1. Associate |transport| with |session|, set its `reliability` to that of |session|, set its state to "`connected`" and resolve its `ready` promise.

</div>

Each construction opens a new [=WebTransport session=]. If both peers construct an {{LP2PQuicTransport}} on the same connection, there are two sessions. To receive the peer's sessions, use an {{LP2PQuicTransportListener}}.

Lifecycle {#lp2p-quic-transport-lifecycle}
-----------------------------------------

An {{LP2PQuicTransport}} and its {{LP2PConnection}} affect each other as follows:

* {{WebTransport/close()}} on the transport runs [=close a WebTransport session=] with its `closeCode` and `reason`. The peer's transport is closed cleanly with them. The {{LP2PConnection}} stays open.
* If the [=WebTransport session=] is closed abruptly or fails, the transport fails with a {{WebTransportError}} whose `source` is "`session`". The {{LP2PConnection}} stays open.
* When the [=WebTransport session=] is drained by the peer with [=drain a WebTransport session=], the transport's `draining` promise resolves.
* When the {{LP2PConnection}} closes, by {{LP2PConnection/close()}}, by the peer or because the transport failed, each of its transports fails with a {{WebTransportError}} whose `source` is "`session`", and its listener is closed, before the {{LP2PConnection/close}} event fires. See [=LP2PConnection/finalize=].
* {{LP2PReceiver/stop()}} affects neither connections already delivered nor their transports and listeners.

LP2PQuicTransportListener Interface {#lp2p-quic-transport-listener}
-------------------------------------------------------------------

The <dfn interface>LP2PQuicTransportListener</dfn> interface receives the [=incoming WebTransport sessions=] of an {{LP2PConnection}}.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
interface LP2PQuicTransportListener {
  constructor(LP2PConnection connection,
              optional LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit options = {});
  readonly attribute ReadableStream incomingTransports;
  undefined close();
};

dictionary LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit : LP2PQuicTransportInit {
};
</pre>

The options of an {{LP2PQuicTransportListener}} apply to each {{LP2PQuicTransport}} it creates.

An {{LP2PQuicTransportListener}} has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">\[[Connection]]</dfn>, an {{LP2PConnection}}; an <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">\[[Options]]</dfn>, an {{LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit}}; an <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">\[[IncomingTransports]]</dfn>, a {{ReadableStream}}; an <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">\[[OwnsStream]]</dfn>, a boolean; and a <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">\[[Closed]]</dfn>, a boolean, initially false. An {{LP2PConnection}} has a <dfn attribute for="LP2PConnection">\[[Listener]]</dfn>, an {{LP2PQuicTransportListener}} or null, initially null.

<div algorithm="LP2PQuicTransportListener constructor">
The <dfn constructor for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">LP2PQuicTransportListener(|connection|, |options|)</dfn> constructor steps are to [=set up an LP2PQuicTransportListener=] [=this=] given |connection| and |options|.

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>set up an LP2PQuicTransportListener</dfn> |listener| given an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection|, |options| and an optional {{ReadableStream}} |shared|:

1. If |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Closed]]}} is true, or |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Listener]]}} is not null, throw an "{{InvalidStateError}}" {{DOMException}}.
1. Set |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[Connection]]}} to |connection| and |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[Options]]}} to |options|.
1. If |shared| is given, set |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[IncomingTransports]]}} to |shared| and |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[OwnsStream]]}} to false. The listener then delivers into |shared|, and its owner closes it.
1. Otherwise, let |stream| be a [=new=] {{ReadableStream}}. [=ReadableStream/Set up=] |stream| with <var ignore>cancelAlgorithm</var> set to an algorithm that [=close an LP2PQuicTransportListener|closes=] |listener|, and <var ignore>highWaterMark</var> set to an [=implementation-defined=] positive number. Set |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[IncomingTransports]]}} to |stream| and |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[OwnsStream]]}} to true.
1. Set |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Listener]]}} to |listener|.

</div>

There is at most one listener per {{LP2PConnection}} at a time.

The <dfn attribute for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">incomingTransports</dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=].{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[IncomingTransports]]}}. Its chunks are {{LP2PQuicTransport}} objects.

<div algorithm="LP2PQuicTransportListener close">
The <dfn method for="LP2PQuicTransportListener">close()</dfn> method steps are to [=close an LP2PQuicTransportListener|close=] [=this=].

</div>

<div algorithm>
To <dfn>close an LP2PQuicTransportListener</dfn> |listener|:

1. If |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[Closed]]}} is true, return.
1. Set |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[Closed]]}} to true.
1. Set |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[Connection]]}}.{{LP2PConnection/[[Listener]]}} to null.
1. If |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[OwnsStream]]}} is true, [=ReadableStream/close=] |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[IncomingTransports]]}}, unless it is already closed or errored. Transports already queued can still be read.

</div>

After {{LP2PQuicTransportListener/close()}}, a new listener can be constructed on the same connection.

<div algorithm="incoming WebTransport session">
When an [=incoming WebTransport session=] |session| is reported on the {{LP2PConnection/[[Session]]}} of an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection|, the user agent must [=queue a global task=] on the [=networking task source=] given |connection|'s [=relevant global object=] to run these steps:

1. Let |listener| be |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Listener]]}}.
1. If |listener| is null, or the [=ReadableStream/desired size to fill up to the high water mark=] of |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[IncomingTransports]]}} is not a positive number, refuse |session| and abort these steps.
1. If |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[Options]]}}["{{LP2PQuicTransportInit/requireUnreliable}}"] is true and |session| is "`reliable-only`", refuse |session| and abort these steps.
1. Let |transport| be a [=new=] {{LP2PQuicTransport}} in |connection|'s [=relevant realm=].
1. [=Initialize an LP2PQuicTransport=] |transport| given |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[Options]]}}, set |transport|.{{LP2PQuicTransport/[[Connection]]}} to |connection| and [=set/append=] |transport| to |connection|.{{LP2PConnection/[[Transports]]}}.
1. Accept |session|, associate |transport| with it, set its `reliability` to that of |session|, set its state to "`connected`" and resolve its `ready` promise.
1. [=ReadableStream/Enqueue=] |transport| into |listener|.{{LP2PQuicTransportListener/[[IncomingTransports]]}}.

</div>

So an [=incoming WebTransport session=] is refused when there is no listener, or when the listener's queue is full because script is not reading it. An [=incoming WebTransport session=] that is reported before its {{LP2PConnection}} is exposed to script, that is, before {{LP2PRequest/start()}} resolves or the {{LP2PReceiver/connection}} event is dispatched, is held until then, so that script can construct a listener first.

Examples {#lp2p-quic-transport-examples}
----------------------------------------

Example: The requester opens a {{LP2PQuicTransport}} and the receiver listens for it:

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
// Receiver
const receiver = new LP2PReceiver({
    nickname: "example-receiver",
});
receiver.onconnection = async e => {
    const listener = new LP2PQuicTransportListener(e.connection);
    for await (const transport of listener.incomingTransports) {
        // Incoming transports are already ready.
        const stream = await transport.createBidirectionalStream();
    }
};
await receiver.start();

// Requester
const request = new LP2PRequest({
    nickname: "example-request",
});
const conn = await request.start();
const transport = new LP2PQuicTransport(conn);
await transport.ready;
</pre>

Either side can open: the receiver can construct an {{LP2PQuicTransport}} on its connection while the requester listens.

Refer to the [WebTransport examples](https://www.w3.org/TR/webtransport/#examples) for usage of a [[!webtransport]] object.

</section>

<section algorithm="lp2p">

Shorthand APIs {#lp2p-namespace}
================================

The <dfn namespace>lp2p</dfn> namespace provides shorthands for the common case of one side making itself discoverable and the other connecting, with WebTransport. They are composed from the APIs above, as if script used them, and add no capability. They cover {{LP2PQuicTransport}} only: a connection over which the peer only opens [=data channels=] never shows up in {{lp2p/makeDiscoverable()}}. On both sides, {{LP2PQuicTransport/connection}} gives access to the {{LP2PConnection}}, for example to open [=data channels=] or close it.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=(Window,Worker), SecureContext]
namespace lp2p {
  ReadableStream makeDiscoverable(optional LP2PMakeDiscoverableOptions options = {});
  Promise&lt;LP2PQuicTransport&gt; connect(optional LP2PConnectOptions options = {});
};

dictionary LP2PMakeDiscoverableOptions : LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit {
  DOMString nickname;
  sequence&lt;USVString&gt; allowedOrigins = [];
};

dictionary LP2PConnectOptions : LP2PQuicTransportInit {
  DOMString nickname;
  AbortSignal signal;
};
</pre>

The members of {{LP2PMakeDiscoverableOptions}} and {{LP2PConnectOptions}} have the meaning of the members of the same name of {{LP2PReceiverOptions}}, {{LP2PRequestOptions}} and {{LP2PRequestStartOptions}}.

<div class="note non-normative">
This is non-normative. {{lp2p/makeDiscoverable()}} is roughly equivalent to the following JavaScript, except that it uses no public methods or attributes, so script can't intercept it, and that its listeners share one stream. Where they differ, the algorithm wins.

<pre highlight='js'>
lp2p.makeDiscoverable = options => {
  const receiver = new LP2PReceiver(options), listeners = new Set();
  let controller;
  const stream = new ReadableStream({ start: c => { controller = c; },
    cancel: () => { receiver.stop(); listeners.forEach(l => l.close()); } });
  receiver.onconnection = async ({ connection }) => {
    const listener = new LP2PQuicTransportListener(connection, options);
    listeners.add(listener);
    for await (const transport of listener.incomingTransports) controller.enqueue(transport);
  };
  receiver.start().catch(e => controller.error(e));
  return stream;
};
</pre>
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="lp2p">makeDiscoverable(|options|)</dfn> method steps are:

1. Let |realm| be the [=current global object=]'s [=relevant realm=].
1. Let |listeners| be an empty [=/set=].
1. Let |receiver| be a [=new=] {{LP2PReceiver}} in |realm|.
1. Let |stream| be a [=new=] {{ReadableStream}} in |realm|. [=ReadableStream/Set up=] |stream| with <var ignore>cancelAlgorithm</var> set to an algorithm that [=stop an LP2PReceiver|stops=] |receiver| and [=close an LP2PQuicTransportListener|closes=] each [=set/item=] of |listeners|, and <var ignore>highWaterMark</var> set to an [=implementation-defined=] positive number.
1. [=Set up an LP2PReceiver=] |receiver| given an {{LP2PReceiverOptions}} with the {{LP2PReceiverOptions/nickname}} and {{LP2PReceiverOptions/allowedOrigins}} members of |options|, and these steps given an {{LP2PConnection}} |connection|: let |listener| be a [=new=] {{LP2PQuicTransportListener}} in |realm|, [=set up an LP2PQuicTransportListener=] |listener| given |connection|, the {{LP2PQuicTransportListenerInit}} members of |options| and |stream|, and [=set/append=] |listener| to |listeners|. Rethrow any exception.
1. [=Upon rejection=] of the result of [=start an LP2PReceiver|starting=] |receiver| with |reason|, [=ReadableStream/error=] |stream| with |reason|.
1. Return |stream|.

</div>

The returned stream's chunks are {{LP2PQuicTransport}} objects, already ready. Each connection's listener enqueues into it, so when script doesn't read it and it is full, further sessions are refused, as described in [[#lp2p-quic-transport-listener]]. {{lp2p/makeDiscoverable()}} owns the listener of each connection it creates, so constructing another {{LP2PQuicTransportListener}} on it throws. Cancelling the stream stops advertising and closes the listeners; as with {{LP2PReceiver/stop()}}, connections already made stay open.

<div class="note non-normative">
This is non-normative. {{lp2p/connect()}} is roughly equivalent to the following JavaScript, except that it uses no public methods or attributes. Where they differ, the algorithm wins.

<pre highlight='js'>
lp2p.connect = async options => {
  const connection = await new LP2PRequest(options).start(options);
  const transport = new LP2PQuicTransport(connection, options);
  try { await transport.ready; } catch (e) { connection.close(); throw e; }
  return transport;
};
</pre>
</div>

<div algorithm>
The <dfn method for="lp2p">connect(|options|)</dfn> method steps are:

1. Let |request| be a [=new=] {{LP2PRequest}} in the [=current global object=]'s [=relevant realm=], with its {{LP2PRequest/[[Nickname]]}} set to |options|["{{LP2PConnectOptions/nickname}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or the empty string otherwise.
1. Let |signal| be |options|["{{LP2PConnectOptions/signal}}"] if it [=map/exists=], or null otherwise.
1. Let |promise| be [=a new promise=].
1. Let |started| be the result of [=start an LP2PRequest|starting=] |request| given |signal|.
1. [=Upon rejection=] of |started| with |reason|, [=reject=] |promise| with |reason|.
1. [=Upon fulfillment=] of |started| with |connection|:
    1. If |signal| is not null and is [=AbortSignal/aborted=], [=close an LP2PConnection|close=] |connection|, [=reject=] |promise| with |signal|'s [=AbortSignal/abort reason=], and abort these steps.
    1. Let |transport| be a [=new=] {{LP2PQuicTransport}} in the [=current global object=]'s [=relevant realm=]. [=Set up an LP2PQuicTransport=] |transport| given |connection| and the {{LP2PQuicTransportInit}} members of |options|. If this throws an exception |error|, [=close an LP2PConnection|close=] |connection|, [=reject=] |promise| with |error|, and abort these steps.
    1. [=Upon fulfillment=] of |transport|'s \[[Ready]] promise, [=resolve=] |promise| with |transport|.
    1. [=Upon rejection=] of it with |reason|, [=close an LP2PConnection|close=] |connection| and [=reject=] |promise| with |reason|.
1. Return |promise|.

</div>

{{lp2p/connect()}} resolves once the user picked a peer and the [=WebTransport session=] is up. It creates no listener, so script can construct one on {{LP2PQuicTransport/connection}}. Aborting {{LP2PConnectOptions/signal}} takes down the picker or the session being set up, as for {{LP2PRequest/start()}}.

Examples {#lp2p-namespace-examples}
-----------------------------------

<pre class='example' highlight='js'>
// Peer A
for await (const transport of lp2p.makeDiscoverable({ nickname: "Peer A" })) {
    const stream = await transport.createBidirectionalStream();
}

// Peer B
const transport = await lp2p.connect({ nickname: "Peer B" });
const channel = transport.connection.createDataChannel("chat");
</pre>

</section>

Security and privacy considerations {#security-and-privacy-considerations}
=========================================================================

Transport bindings {#security-osp}
------------------------------------
The security and privacy considerations of the protocol underlying the [=transport binding=] in use must be considered when implementing this API. For [[#osp-binding]], these are the Security and Privacy considerations of the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]].

Personally identifiable information {#security-pii}
---------------------------------------------------
No peer attributes reach origins: the [=peer/peer identity=], [=peer/display name=], [=peer/nickname=] and [=peer/capabilities=] are only used by the user agent. Its user interface shows the [=peer/display name=] and [=peer/nickname=] to the user, to pick or accept a peer.

No information is exposed to the origin during service discovery. No IP information is exposed to an origin. This is fully managed by the user agent.

Issue(WICG/local-peer-to-peer#15): Refine the peer attributes exposed to origins.

User interface guidelines {#security-ui}
----------------------------------------

When the user is asked to grant permission to connect to a peer, the user agent should make it clear what origin the request is coming from.

Issue(WICG/local-peer-to-peer#15): Define peer filtering to give the user additional context.

Impact on same-origin policy {#security-same-origin}
----------------------------------------

This document extends the Web platform with the ability to set up real-time, direct communication between browsers and other devices, including other browsers, within a [=local communication medium=].

This means that data and media can be shared between applications running in different browsers, or between an application running in the browser and another user agent that is not a browser such as a headless service provided by a smart TV or smart fridge. This extends the usual barriers in the Web's security model that prevents sending data between entities with different origins.

To limit this, a session is only delivered to an {{LP2PReceiver}} of the same origin as its [=protocol/requesting origin=], unless the receiver opts in to other origins with {{LP2PReceiverOptions/allowedOrigins}}. The [=protocol/requesting origin=] is asserted by the peer's user agent, so a peer can claim any origin; but a lying peer can only be one the user already [=/authenticated=] and trusts with what they send it.

Device Access {#security-device-access}
---------------------------------------

The Local Peer-to-Peer API requires user permission for a page to access any peers. The API uses purpose-fit protocols for data communication. It cannot be used to connect to raw sockets or unknowing HTTP servers. As in {{LP2PRequest/start()}}, the user first picks a peer in [=acquire a local peer grant=]; the session with it is then [=protocol/authenticated=], with a [=trust ceremony=] if needed, and accepted by the peer; only then is the grant recorded with [=grant a peer=].

Persistent State {#security-persistent-state}
---------------------------------------------
The persistent state rules of the [=transport binding=] in use must be followed when implementing this API. For [[#osp-binding]], these are the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] [Persistent State rules](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#persistent-state).

Secure Contexts {#security-secure-context}
------------------------------------------
The Local Peer-to-Peer API must only be provided in a secure context.

Binding to the Open Screen Network Protocol {#osp-binding}
==========================================================

This section defines a [=transport binding=] to the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] (OSP). In this binding, a [=peer=] is an OSP agent and a [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] is an authenticated [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] connection between two agents. Each [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] has its own QUIC connection, even with the same agent, so that sessions are not shared between origins.

To <dfn>discover metadata</dfn> of an agent, the user agent follows [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] [transport and metadata discovery](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#transport).

The concepts and operations of [[#transport-abstraction]] map onto OSP as follows:

<table class="data">
 <thead>
  <tr>
   <th>concept or operation
   <th>OSP binding
  </tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody>
  <tr>
   <td>[=start advertising=], [=stop advertising=], [=discover peers=], [=stop discovering peers=]
   <td>[[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]]
   [discovery](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#discovery)
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=peer/peer identity=]
   <td>The [agent fingerprint](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#computing-agent-fingerprint)
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=trust anchor=]
   <td>The key of the peer's agent certificate, scoped to [=trust anchor/scope/one peer=]: the peer is [=self-anchored=]. An agent certificate with a new fingerprint needs a new [=trust ceremony=].
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=peer/display name=]
   <td>The display name from the [agent-info-response](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#agent-info-response) message, obtained by [=discover metadata=]
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=peer/nickname=]
   <td>Not yet carried.
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=peer/capabilities=]
   <td>The capabilities from the [agent-info-response](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#agent-info-response) message, obtained by [=discover metadata=]: [=data-channels=] for [=peer/capabilities/data channels=] and [=quic-transport=] for [=peer/capabilities/WebTransport=]
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=protocol/client=], [=protocol/server=]
   <td>The [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] client and the [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] server of the connection
  </tr>
  <tr>
   <td>[=protocol/requesting origin=], [=accept=], [=refuse=]
   <td>[[#session-extension]]
