Issue: Define how the [=peer/nickname=] is carried and how a [=data channel=] is closed.

Part of these operations are defined below as [protocol extensions](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#protocol-extensions) to the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]].

Session extension {#session-extension}
--------------------------------------

To [=establish a session=] with an agent given an origin, the [=protocol/client=] opens a new QUIC connection to the agent, as described in [transport](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#transport). Both agents send auth-capabilities when the connection starts, as in OSP, so that both know which agent presents the PSK; this message does not start authentication. Only the [=protocol/client=] starts [authentication](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#authentication), although OSP otherwise has an agent initiate authentication with an agent it has not verified.

Issue: When the [=protocol/client=] is the PSK consumer, it starts authentication with `psk-needs-presentation`. As written, OSP requires that message to carry `pA`, which needs the PSK the presenter has not created yet. This binding assumes OSP is fixed so that the message carries no `public-value`, and the presenter then starts the presenter-initiated flow.

This binding conveys the [=protocol/requesting origin=] only after authentication:

* Once it has received the agent's agent-info-response, by [=discover metadata=] on that connection, the [=protocol/client=] starts authentication if it has not verified the [=protocol/server=]'s agent certificate.
* Once it has verified it, the [=protocol/client=] sends an [=lp2p-session-request=] message on a new bidirectional QUIC stream.
* A [=protocol/server=] that has not verified the [=protocol/client=]'s agent certificate answers with a provisional [=lp2p-session-response=] with the result `authentication-required`, without looking at the requesting-origin, and the [=protocol/client=] then starts authentication. The [=protocol/server=] sends no authentication message other than auth-capabilities before the [=protocol/client=] does.
* Once it has verified the [=protocol/client=]'s agent certificate, the [=protocol/server=] reports the connection as an [=incoming session=], with the requesting-origin as its [=protocol/requesting origin=].

So the requesting-origin is only sent to a verified [=protocol/server=], and the [=protocol/server=]'s answer about it only reaches a verified [=protocol/client=]. Agents that have verified each other run no [=trust ceremony=].

The [=lp2p-session-request=] contains:

: requesting-origin
:: The [=ASCII serialization of an origin|ASCII serialization=] of the origin. The [=protocol/server=] uses the [=url/origin=] of the result of running the [=URL parser=] on this value, or an [=opaque origin=] if parsing fails.

A [=protocol/client=] sends [=lp2p-session-request=] only to an agent whose [=peer/capabilities=] are not empty, and only once per QUIC connection. A [=protocol/server=] that receives a second one closes the connection.

Authentication verifies the agent certificate that each agent presented in the TLS handshake of the QUIC connection that carries the [=lp2p-session-request=]: SPAKE2 uses the fingerprints of those certificates, and a certificate verified earlier is bound by the handshake itself. So the requesting-origin is bound to the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=].

The [=protocol/server=] answers on the same stream with an [=lp2p-session-response=] message, containing:

: result
:: `authentication-required`, provisional, as above; a final result follows on the same stream. `success` if the [=protocol/server=] [=accepts=] the session, or `permanent-error` if it [=refuses=] it. The [=protocol/server=] sends either only once it has verified the [=protocol/client=]'s agent certificate. After a refusal, the [=protocol/client=] ends its side of the stream, and the [=protocol/server=] closes the QUIC connection once it has, or after an [=implementation-defined=] timeout, so that the response is not lost.

The [=protocol/client=] treats the session as [=accepted=] once it has received `success` and has also verified the [=protocol/server=]'s agent certificate. Both agents then end their side of the stream.

The [=protocol/server=] does not send messages of the other extensions in this section on the connection before it has sent `success`, and the [=protocol/client=] not before it treats the session as [=accepted=]. The [=protocol/server=] may open data channels and [=WebTransport sessions=] right after sending `success`; the [=protocol/client=] holds streams that arrive before it treats the session as [=accepted=] until it does, and discards them if the session is not. To [=close a session=], an agent closes the QUIC connection.

Data channel extension {#data-channel-extension}
------------------------------------------------

In order to signal support for this protocol extension, the agent should include the [=data-channels=] [agent-capability](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#appendix-a) as part of the [agent-info-response](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#agent-info-response) message exchanged during [=discover metadata=]. An agent does not send [=data-channel-open-request=] to an agent whose [=peer/capabilities=] lack [=peer/capabilities/data channels=].

To [=open a data channel=], an agent may send a [=data-channel-open-request=] message on a new [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] stream. The [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] must be authenticated. The message must contain the following values:

: channel-id
:: An ID number between 0 and 65,534 which uniquely identifies the data channel. When the binding chooses the id, the [=protocol/client=] uses an even number and the [=protocol/server=] an odd number. The value 65,535 is reserved, as for {{RTCDataChannel}}; {{LP2PDataChannel/id}} is an `unsigned short` for parity with {{RTCDataChannel/id}}, but cannot take that value.

: label
:: a string that contains a name describing the data channel. These labels are not required to be unique.

: protocol
:: a string containing the name of the subprotocol in use. If no protocol was specified when the data channel was created, then this property's value is the empty string ("").

The receiving agent reports the [=data-channel-open-request=] as an [=incoming data channel=]. When the receiver receives the [=data-channel-open-request=], it should send back a [=data-channel-open-response=] message. The response must include the following:

: result
:: a code indicating success or failure, and the reason for the failure. If the user agent refuses the [=incoming data channel=], the result indicates failure.

If the [=data-channel-open-response=] message indicates success, the data channel is considered open. Agents can now [=send data on a channel=] by sending [data-frame](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#data-frame) messages (type key 24) on the same [[!RFC9000|QUIC]] stream the data channel was opened. The message must include the following keys; the other keys of [data-frame](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#data-frame) (1, 2, 3 and 5) are unused, should not be sent, and must be ignored by the receiving agent:

: encoding-id
:: Determines the encoding of the data being sent. The values are specified as [=data-channel-encoding-id=], instead of referencing a data encoding offer:
    0: [=encoding-id-blob|Blob=];
    1: [=encoding-id-string|String=];
    2: [=encoding-id-array-buffer|ArrayBuffer=].

: payload
:: The binary representation of the data being sent.

A text message is sent with [=encoding-id-string|String=]; a binary message with [=encoding-id-blob|Blob=] or [=encoding-id-array-buffer|ArrayBuffer=]. The receiving agent reports each [data-frame](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#data-frame) as a [=received message=], which is text if its encoding-id is [=encoding-id-string|String=] and binary otherwise.

WebTransport extension {#webtransport-extension}
------------------------------------------------

The protocol extension to [=send data on a channel=] provides an ergonomic way to send simple messages. An agent can [=open a WebTransport session=] for communication that requires low overhead and more granular streams control.

An agent that supports this extension includes the [=quic-transport=] [agent-capability](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#appendix-a) in its [agent-info-response](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#agent-info-response), obtained by [=discover metadata=], and must support QUIC DATAGRAM frames [[!RFC9221]] on each connection that carries [=WebTransport sessions=], so that its sessions are "`supports-unreliable`". An agent must not send the messages of this extension to an agent whose [=peer/capabilities=] lack [=peer/capabilities/WebTransport=], since an unknown type key closes the whole connection; [=open a WebTransport session=] fails instead. The capability describes the agent, not an origin: whether a page takes a session is answered by [=data-transport-start-response=].

A [=WebTransport session=] is carried either on the QUIC connection of its [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], next to the other streams of that connection, or on a <dfn>dedicated connection</dfn>. Either agent can open a session on the QUIC connection of the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]; only the [=protocol/client=] opens a [=dedicated connection=]. So both agents can [=open a WebTransport session=], as [[#transport-concepts]] requires.

### Sessions ### {#webtransport-sessions}

To [=open a WebTransport session=], an agent opens a bidirectional QUIC stream, the session's <dfn>control stream</dfn>, and sends a [=data-transport-start-request=] message on it, containing:

: exchange-id
:: A number below 2<sup>62</sup> that identifies the session within its [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], across that session's QUIC connection and [=dedicated connections=]. The [=protocol/client=] uses even numbers and the [=protocol/server=] odd numbers, and neither reuses one.

: session-binding
:: Only on a [=dedicated connection=], see [[#webtransport-dedicated]].

The other agent reports an [=incoming WebTransport session=]. On the same stream it answers with a [=data-transport-start-response=] message, whose result is `success` if the session is accepted. Otherwise, it sends `permanent-error` and ends the stream with FIN; the session never opens. The opening agent does not open streams or send datagrams for the session before it receives `success`, and abandons a session it has had no response for by resetting the [=control stream=].

### Streams and datagrams ### {#webtransport-streams}

Each WebTransport stream is a QUIC stream with the same directionality, opened on the connection that carries the session. Its first message, sent by the agent that opened it, is a [=data-transport-stream=] message with the session's exchange-id. The WebTransport stream's data follows right after it, the other direction of a bidirectional stream carries only data, and FIN ends the stream. No response is sent.

A WebTransport datagram is a QUIC DATAGRAM frame whose payload is the session's exchange-id, encoded as a QUIC variable-length integer [[!RFC9000]], followed by the datagram. Datagrams for a session that is not open are dropped.

A WebTransport stream is reset or stopped with RESET_STREAM or STOP_SENDING. The QUIC application error code is the WebTransport stream error code, from 0 to 2<sup>32</sup>&minus;1. Codes from 2<sup>32</sup> are reserved for this binding:

: <dfn>session-gone</dfn> (2<sup>32</sup>)
:: The stream's session is closed or unknown. It is reported to script without a stream error code.

An unknown code from 2<sup>32</sup> is treated as [=session-gone=]. An agent may hold, for a short time, a stream for a session that is not yet open, such as one the accepting agent opened right after sending `success`. Otherwise it refuses the stream with STOP_SENDING, and for a bidirectional stream also RESET_STREAM, with [=session-gone=]. A stream reset before its first message arrived is discarded.

### Close and drain ### {#webtransport-close}

To [=close a WebTransport session=], an agent sends a [=data-transport-close=] message on the [=control stream=] and then ends the stream with FIN. It contains:

: close-code
:: The close code, from 0 to 2<sup>32</sup>&minus;1.

: reason
:: The reason, UTF-8 encoded, at most 1,024 bytes.

An agent that receives [=data-transport-close=] or a FIN on the [=control stream=] ends its side of it too. Both agents then reset and stop all streams of the session with [=session-gone=]. A [=control stream=] that ends with FIN without a [=data-transport-close=] closes the session with code 0 and an empty reason. A [=control stream=] that is reset, or a connection that closes, closes the session abruptly. So an agent that sent [=data-transport-close=] closes the QUIC connection only once the peer has ended its side of the [=control stream=], or after an [=implementation-defined=] timeout, so that the message is not lost.

To [=drain a WebTransport session=], an agent sends a [=data-transport-drain=] message on the [=control stream=]. An agent may do so when it intends to close the session soon.

### Keying material exporters ### {#webtransport-exporters}

To [=export keying material=] from a [=WebTransport session=], an agent uses the TLS exporter of the QUIC connection that carries the session, with the label "`EXPORTER-WebTransport`" and, as context, the WebTransport Exporter Context of [WebTransport over HTTP/3, Section 4.8](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-webtrans-http3#section-4.8), with the exchange-id as its session ID.

### Dedicated connections ### {#webtransport-dedicated}

The [=protocol/client=] may carry a [=WebTransport session=] on a [=dedicated connection=] instead, for example to give it its own congestion control. It opens a new QUIC connection to the agent with the ALPN token "`q2q`" and the same agent certificate as its [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=]. The [=protocol/client=] closes the connection unless the server's agent certificate has the [agent fingerprint](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#computing-agent-fingerprint) of the peer of that session. The [=protocol/server=] closes it unless the client's agent certificate has the fingerprint of the peer of one of its [=Local Peer-to-Peer sessions=].

Each [=data-transport-start-request=] on a [=dedicated connection=] contains a session-binding: 32 bytes from the TLS exporter of the QUIC connection of the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=], with the label "`EXPORTER-LP2P-q2q`" and an empty context. The first one binds the connection to the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] whose exporter yields the same bytes. The [=protocol/server=] closes the connection if there is none, or if a later request carries a different session-binding.

Only the [=protocol/client=] opens sessions on a [=dedicated connection=], and only the messages of this extension are sent on it, without agent-info or authentication messages. Everything else in this section applies unchanged. The [=protocol/client=] closes the connection when its last session closes, or when the [=Local Peer-to-Peer session=] closes.

Appendix A: OSP Extension Messages {#appendix-a}
================================================

The following messages are defined according to and as an extension to the [[!openscreen-network|Open Screen Network Protocol]] [Messages](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-network/#appendix-a). The `agent-capability`, `data-frame`, `request`, `response` and `result` types are defined in the [[!openscreen-application|Open Screen Application Protocol]] [Messages](https://www.w3.org/TR/openscreen-application/#appendix-a).

Note: The type keys and capability IDs for these extensions are not officially registered yet. They will be registered as this specification matures. 

<pre class="data" highlight='cddl'>
agent-capability /= &(
  <dfn>data-channels</dfn>: 1100
  <dfn>quic-transport</dfn>: 1200
)

<dfn>data-channel-encoding-id</dfn> = &(
  <dfn>encoding-id-blob</dfn>: 0
  <dfn>encoding-id-string</dfn>: 1
  <dfn>encoding-id-array-buffer</dfn>: 2
)

; data-frame (type key 24) is the Open Screen Application Protocol message;
; only its encoding-id (0) and payload (4) keys are used.

; type key 1110
<dfn>lp2p-session-request</dfn> = {
  request
  1: text ; requesting-origin
}

; type key 1111
<dfn>lp2p-session-response</dfn> = {
  response
  1: lp2p-session-result ; result
}

lp2p-session-result = &amp;(
  result,
  authentication-required: 1110
)

; type key 1101
<dfn>data-channel-open-request</dfn> = {
  request
  1: uint ; channel-id
  2: text ; label
  3: text ; protocol
}

; type key 1102
<dfn>data-channel-open-response</dfn> = {
  response
  1: &amp;result ; result
}

; type key 1201
<dfn>data-transport-start-request</dfn> = {
  request
  1: exchange-id ; exchange-id
  ? 2: bytes .size 32 ; session-binding
}

; type key 1202
<dfn>data-transport-start-response</dfn> = {
  response
  1: &amp;result ; result
}

; type key 1203
<dfn>data-transport-stream</dfn> = {
  1: exchange-id ; exchange-id
}

exchange-id = uint .lt 4611686018427387904 ; below 2^62

; type key 1204 is reserved.

; type key 1205
<dfn>data-transport-close</dfn> = {
  0: uint .size 4 ; close-code
  1: text .size (0..1024) ; reason
}

; type key 1206
<dfn>data-transport-drain</dfn> = {
}
</pre>